<p>An application can ask for three orders and perform six database reads. It can replace those reads with one join and transfer the same order dozens of times. It can even send an update while executing what looks like a read. The application expression tells you what the programmer asked the library to do; the executed statements and consumed results tell you what work followed.</p>
<p>Handwritten SQL, query builders and <strong>object-relational mappers</strong> (ORMs) all need that check. An ORM represents rows as objects and relationships as object references or collections. A query builder constructs SQL from language expressions. Either can make ordinary code easier to maintain, provided the operation still has a clear result, authority and work budget.</p>
<p>This article extends the <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/">application request</a> into the shop’s staff-facing order list. Authorised staff can see tenant 7’s orders, including Mina’s order 103 from the parent and Jo’s orders 101 and 102. We compare three ways to produce the same response. The concrete library checks use SQLAlchemy 2.0.44, pg8000 1.31.5 and PostgreSQL 18.6; other libraries require their own traces. The figures are explanatory models of those mechanisms.</p>
