<h2 id="trace-one-request-to-its-actual-statements">Trace one request to its actual statements</h2>
<p>Write the required response before choosing a loading method. Here it is the first three authorised orders by identifier, with each customer’s display name and every item’s identifier and SKU, ordered by item identifier. An order without items must remain, with an empty array. This small fixture contains five items and two distinct customers:</p>
<Code code={response} lang="json" themes={themes}/>
<p>The order is deliberately simple so loading behaviour stays visible. A real endpoint should carry the <a href="/review/relational-next/sql/#order-and-page-a-result-deliberately">SQL pagination contract</a> through unchanged. Its parent page, child collections and response bytes all need limits.</p>
<p>Capture work from handler entry until the response has been constructed, including templates, serialisers and property getters. Correlate each record with the request, transaction and owning connection. In SQLAlchemy, cursor execution events expose SQL sent through the engine; transaction events record begin, commit and rollback separately. They do not automatically count fetches performed inside a server-side cursor or all wire exchanges.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Different measures of one application operation"><table><thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>What to count</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>SQL statements</td><td>The selects and changes actually executed, including automatic work. Keep transaction-control commands visible separately.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Database round trips</td><td>Request/reply waits in the driver or protocol. Preparation, execution, fetches and transaction completion can add waits.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Returned rows</td><td>Rows crossing the database boundary before ORM deduplication. Record an unknown count as unknown.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Materialised and retained data</td><td>Entities, result mappings and response objects created, then those still reachable. Measure bytes where row width or response size matters.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Our instrumentation observed six select statements for the lazy three-order response, but twenty-two driver synchronisation waits including transaction begin and rollback. One joined select used seven waits on this pinned stack. pg8000’s bound-statement path separates protocol stages; a different driver can combine them. These are counts of waits for PostgreSQL’s ready response, not packet counts or latency measurements. “One query” is not a universal network unit.</p>
<p>Record statement shape, parameter types, safe operation identifiers and counts. Redact personal and secret values by default; an execution plan investigation may need selected values under controlled access. Avoid interpolating values into SQL just to make a readable log. Logging only the ORM method name would miss the very work being investigated.</p>

<h2 id="find-the-reads-hidden-behind-object-access">Find the reads hidden behind object access</h2>
<p>A <strong>lazy load</strong> fetches a relationship when code first accesses it. This SQLAlchemy example loads the order objects first, then reads their relationships while constructing the response:</p>
<Code code={lazy} lang="python" themes={themes}/>
<p>Step through Figure 1 with lazy relationships. Watch order 102: it shares Jo with order 101, but has no items. Then switch to loading the relationships before serialising.</p>
<WorkPlayer scene="lazy" number={1}/>
<p>The default lazy path issued one order query, two customer queries and three item queries. SQLAlchemy’s <strong>identity map</strong> keeps one entity instance per identity within the Session; the simple customer reference could reuse Jo locally. The empty item collection still needed a query. Serialising the same already-loaded objects again issued no further reads.</p>
<p>The repeated collection reads are the <strong>N+1 problem</strong>: one parent query followed by a query for each of N parents. More than one relationship can add further repeats. The native fixture used one, three and six orders; complete response construction issued three, six and ten selects respectively. A fixed three-query loading plan and a per-order loading plan can look identical on a one-order test.</p>
<p>Test a fresh request scope as well as a warm one. A relationship populated by earlier work can hide a missing load, and expiration can make a later access issue SQL again. Make required relationships explicit and, where supported, reject unexpected loads. SQLAlchemy’s <code>raiseload("*")</code> raised on the first unloaded relationship in our check. It guards relationship access; it does not prohibit the ORM’s internal flush work, and deferred columns need their own guard.</p>

<h2 id="choose-a-loading-shape-for-the-response">Choose a loading shape for the response</h2>
<p>A <strong>projection</strong> selects only the fields the response needs. The SQL below returns order and customer fields beside each item, keeping an empty order through the left join. The worksheet also expresses this projection with SQLAlchemy’s query builder, using mapped columns without loading tracked entity objects.</p>
<Sql code={projected} label="SQLAlchemy text() form: named values stay separate from the SQL"/>
<p>Assemble the response by parent identity, appending an item only when <code>item_id</code> is present. Order 102’s null-extended row means an empty collection, not an item with a null identifier. The <a href="/review/relational-next/joins-and-aggregation/#unmatched">join explanation</a> develops that meaning.</p>
<p>A <strong>joined eager load</strong> fetches related entities in the initial select. A <strong>select-IN load</strong> fetches relationships in later selects for a set of parent keys. The measured versions produced exactly the response above:</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Observed loading shapes for three orders"><table><thead><tr><th>Shape</th><th>Selects</th><th>Returned rows</th><th>Tracked entities retained before disposal</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Projection and explicit assembly</td><td>1</td><td>6</td><td>0</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Joined customer and items</td><td>1</td><td>6</td><td>10</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Separate orders, customers and items</td><td>3</td><td>10</td><td>10</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Six joined rows represent two items for 101, one null-extended row for 102 and three items for 103. Ten entities means three orders, two customers and five items. The projection still allocates result mappings and the response’s three order and five item objects; zero tracked entities does not mean zero allocation.</p>
<p>Use the projection when the operation needs these fields rather than a navigable entity graph. A join avoids later statement waits and often suits a required many-to-one relationship. Separate collection loads avoid repeating wide parent fields. None is automatically cheapest: compare cardinality, returned columns, transfer, allocation and the response’s consistency requirement.</p>

<h3 id="see-what-joined-collections-multiply">See what joined collections multiply</h3>
<p>Now include parcels as well as items. Order 103 has three items and four parcels, each related independently to the order. Before playing Figure 2, predict how many SQL rows joining both collections returns. Increase the item count after reaching the reconstruction step.</p>
<WorkPlayer scene="multiply" number={2}/>
<p>The join returns twelve combinations for that order. Each item repeats four times. SQLAlchemy’s joined collection result requires <code>unique()</code>; the verified two-collection page reconstructed the right items and parcels with no duplicate entities, but seventeen SQL rows had already crossed the boundary for its three orders. Object deduplication does not establish a transfer bound.</p>
<p>Loading independent collections separately changes a product into separate sums of rows. It may repeat fewer bytes even when the raw row totals are close, because each parent’s fields no longer travel beside every combination. Preserve each collection’s order and identity while assembling it. If the required relationship is actually item-to-parcel, model and query that relationship instead; a faster answer to a different question is still wrong. <a href="/review/relational-next/joins-and-aggregation/#independent-totals">Independent join inputs</a> explains the row arithmetic, and <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">performance diagnosis</a> owns expensive execution inside each statement.</p>

<h3 id="bound-separate-loads-and-batches">Bound separate loads and batches</h3>
<p>The select-IN version declares what response construction may access:</p>
<Code code={load} lang="python" themes={themes}/>
<p>For this page it issued three selects. That count is fixed only within a batch boundary. Our installed SQLAlchemy version groups up to 500 parent identities per select-IN batch: a separate 501-parent check observed item-loader parameter groups of 500 and 1. With composite keys, each identity needs several parameters. PostgreSQL 18 documents a limit of 65,535 query parameters, which pg8000’s documentation also identifies as its protocol limit. We checked the 500-key library threshold, not the protocol maximum; practical statement and result sizes can impose earlier limits. Do not treat a database maximum as a sensible page size.</p>
<p>A hand-built batched loader has the same obligations. Take its identifiers from the bounded, authorised parent result. Initialise every parent’s collection, group child rows by their parent key and retain the parent page’s order. Do not zip two result lists together or drop parents absent from the child result. If a batch is empty, return the empty relationships without issuing a meaningless load.</p>
<p>Several statements can observe changes between them. At PostgreSQL’s default Read Committed level, placing the selects in one transaction does not give them one fixed snapshot. If the response promises a consistent view across its components, use the <a href="/review/relational-next/isolation/#establish-when-the-transaction-sees-a-change">transaction branch’s visibility contract</a> and test it. If changing data between loads is acceptable, say what the response guarantees. A loading strategy cannot choose that product requirement for you.</p>

<h2 id="keep-pages-and-streams-bounded-through-materialisation">Keep pages and streams bounded through materialisation</h2>
<p>A limit in generated SQL may apply to joined rows rather than parent orders. Figure 3 keeps the requested page at three and changes where the limit is applied.</p>
<WorkPlayer scene="page" number={3}/>
<p>Limiting the explicit joined query returned only orders 101 and 102 in the native check. Selecting the parent page first retained 101, 102 and 103 with complete item collections. SQLAlchemy’s <code>joinedload</code> applied to a limited parent select wrapped that select before adding its eager joins in our run. An explicit <code>join</code> followed by <code>limit</code> did not make the same promise. Inspect the generated shape instead of transferring a guarantee between APIs.</p>
<p>The bounded parent page still permits one order with twelve, twelve thousand or more items. If the interface needs only an item count or a preview, project that answer explicitly and label a preview as partial. Otherwise enforce a meaningful maximum, offer a separately paged item operation or refuse an oversized response. Keep those choices consistent with what “items” promises the caller.</p>
<p>A large sequential export has another boundary: <strong>materialisation</strong>, turning database rows into language objects. An iterator can sit on top of an already-buffered driver result. Even a real server-side cursor can feed an application list that retains everything. Figure 4 changes only whether earlier chunks remain reachable.</p>
<WorkPlayer scene="stream" number={4}/>
<Code code={exportCode} lang="python" themes={themes}/>
<p>With this pinned stack, <code>yield_per=100</code> used a server-side cursor. Our protocol observation saw its declaration and bounded fetches. Across 1,200 simple entities, consuming and releasing each chunk left 100 tracked entities at each sampled chunk boundary. Appending every chunk to a list left 1,200 at the end. These are entity-reference observations, not a total process-memory benchmark; buffers, strings, response encoding and the sink also consume memory.</p>
<p>Check support for the particular query and driver: SQLAlchemy’s streaming option is incompatible with joined eager loading of collections and with result-wide <code>unique()</code>. Calling <code>all()</code> also defeats incremental consumption. Keep result consumption inside the owning connection and transaction lifetime. <a href="/review/relational-next/connections/#keep-result-consumption-inside-the-resource-lifetime">Connection lifetimes</a> handles early exit, cleanup and slow consumers; the <a href="/review/relational-next/sql/#order-and-page-a-result-deliberately">pagination article</a> owns the ordering and continuation contract.</p>

<h2 id="inspect-the-writes-and-transactions-an-abstraction-introduces">Inspect the writes and transactions an abstraction introduces</h2>
<p>An ORM can track a changed entity and send its update later. SQLAlchemy’s default <strong>autoflush</strong> sends pending changes before ORM query execution. Figure 5 changes order 101 in memory, then asks for a count. Compare default autoflush with delaying it around that read.</p>
<WorkPlayer scene="flush" number={5}/>
<p>The native trace recorded select, update, select on the writer’s connection. The writer read its changed status, while a separate connection still read <code>paid</code>. Rollback discarded the uncommitted update. <strong>Flush</strong> sends pending writes into the transaction; <strong>commit</strong> completes that transaction. Delaying autoflush does not prevent a later commit from flushing. A read-only endpoint should assert that it sent no writes, rather than infer that from its last method call.</p>
<p>Inspect update predicates as well as the verbs. This fixture configures SQLAlchemy’s <code>version_id_col</code>, so an update checks the loaded version and advances it. Two Sessions loaded the same order; after the first committed, the second flush raised <code>StaleDataError</code>. Version checking is configured behaviour, not protection every ORM update gets automatically. Bulk and raw SQL paths need their own concurrency conditions.</p>
<p>A raw update in the worksheet also left an already-loaded object showing the previous status until it was expired and reloaded. Some ORM bulk APIs synchronise tracked state; a raw statement need not. Decide which representation subsequent code can trust. Pass the owning Session or transaction handle through helpers, inspect automatic transaction starts and completion, and keep error recovery with <a href="/review/relational-next/request-recovery/">request recovery</a> and lifetime cleanup with <a href="/review/relational-next/connections/">connections</a>.</p>

<h2 id="preserve-binding-and-authority-as-queries-are-composed">Preserve binding and authority as queries are composed</h2>
<p>The staff list’s verified tenant-wide permission differs from Jo’s customer-only list. Changing the operation changes the scope. This customer-facing outline applies tenant and customer restrictions both when selecting parents and when loading their items:</p>
<Code code={authority} lang="python" themes={themes}/>
<p>The equality and membership expressions bind values. A raw SQL fragment must use the library’s binding API too: SQLAlchemy’s <code>text("… tenant_id=:tenant")</code> with a separate parameter mapping retains that separation. Choose dynamic sort expressions from application-owned allowed columns, as the <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/#keep-untrusted-input-out-of-sql-structure">parent’s structure rule</a> requires. Do not paste a request’s fragment into an otherwise bound query.</p>
<p>Reusable filters must preserve their grouping: tenant scope <code>AND (paid OR packed)</code> differs from <code>(tenant scope AND paid) OR packed</code>. Optional filters, aliases and library-wide scopes can change which paths receive a restriction. In SQLAlchemy, loader criteria can propagate entity restrictions to relationship loads, but raw SQL and preloaded state still need inspection. Test the actual composed query; a query-builder name does not establish authority. The <a href="/review/relational-next/sql-values/#follow-unknown-through-a-predicate">SQL values article</a> owns null and predicate semantics.</p>
<p>In the native customer-scope check, tenant 7/customer 1 supplied tenant 8’s order 800. The read returned no order, the count was zero, the child query returned no items and the scoped mutation changed no rows. A mixed child request for 101 and 800 returned only 101’s items. Repeat that test for every loader, count, change and raw-query path. Authorise before fetching sensitive rows, and keep Sessions within the operation’s authority boundary so a previously loaded object cannot substitute for a newly scoped lookup.</p>

<h2 id="test-the-generated-work-as-part-of-the-operation-s-contract">Test the generated work as part of the operation’s contract</h2>
<p>Assert response meaning and work bounds together, through the real database, driver and abstraction, including response construction. The ordinary case is insufficient: the fixture should force the loading decisions to matter.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Integration checks for the order-list contract"><table><thead><tr><th>Fixture change</th><th>Check</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>No matching orders; one order with no items</td><td>Empty response or empty collection as required; no disappearing parent or fabricated null item.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>One, three, then many parents</td><td>Expected select/batch range holds through serialisation; no per-parent growth hidden by one-row tests.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Shared customers; fresh and warm Sessions</td><td>Identity reuse does not hide a missing load; response values stay correct.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>One large item collection; two independent collections</td><td>Returned rows, retained entities and response size stay within policy; deduplication preserves each item exactly once.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Another tenant’s identifier in every path</td><td>Read, loader, count and mutation deny access before sensitive data is returned.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Pending entity change before a read</td><td>No unexpected write in a read-only operation; intended updates carry required concurrency conditions.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Export sink retains earlier chunks</td><td>The test detects growing references; the accepted sink stays within its retention bound.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Set counts from the selected loading plan and batch thresholds, allowing documented variation. Assert ordered identifiers and nested results, statement categories, permitted count ranges and observable row or object bounds. Full SQL snapshots alone are brittle and can pass while response construction issues extra work. Recheck after loading changes and dependency upgrades.</p>
<p>The retained <a href={worksheetUrl} download="generated-queries-verify.py">native worksheet</a> and <a href={resultsUrl} download="generated-queries-results.json">observed results</a> cover the mechanisms stated here, with no session setting overrides. They exercise Python response construction, not an HTTP framework, a production serialiser or a complete authorisation service. The fixtures are invented; the counts and exceptions are observed on the pinned versions. Streaming’s initial row count was unknown, so the worksheet counted consumed rows and driver fetch waits instead.</p>
<p>A small number of statements can still scan, sort or transfer far too much. Once the operation’s generated work is visible and its response contract is correct, pass individually costly statements, safe parameter evidence and workload context to <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">performance diagnosis</a>. Reducing a count is useful only when the complete operation improves within its required meaning and bounds.</p>

